I see! `oidc-client-ts` has:
```javascript
import jwt_decode from "jwt-decode";
...
  static decode(token) {
    try {
      return jwt_decode(token);
    } catch (err) {
      Logger.error("JwtUtils.decode", err);
      throw err;
    }
  }
```
And since `oidc-client-ts` imports `jwt-decode` as a default import: `import jwt_decode from "jwt-decode";`, and under ESModules (which `dist/esm/oidc-client-ts.js` is using), `jwt_decode` has no default export anymore! But wait, in Jest/node, `oidc-client-ts` might be importing from `dist/umd/` or `dist/esm/`. Let's check `dist/umd/oidc-client-ts.js`. Let's read `umd/oidc-client-ts.js` around line 238 to see how it requires `"jwt-decode"`.

Ah! The `id_token` is `"valid.id.token"`.
When `"valid.id.token"` is passed to the real `base64UrlDecode`/`jwtDecode`:
The second part is `"id"`.
When `"id"` is decoded, it is `atob("id")` -> `\x89\xdd`.
Then `JSON.parse("\x89\xdd")` fails with `Unexpected token '\x89', "\x89" is not valid JSON`!
This means `jwtDecode` WAS NOT MOCKED when it was called inside `oidc-client-ts`'s `JwtUtils.decode`!
Let's figure out why.
In `umd` version or `esm` version?
Wait! In `node_modules/oidc-client-ts/dist/umd/oidc-client-ts.js`:
```javascript
// src/utils/JwtUtils.ts
